Packaging layout and release procedure
=======================================

Packaging branch for camlab-rpi, kept separate from app source under a
DEP-14 layout:

 * main - app source (camlab/, scripts/, deploy/, install.sh).
   Tagged v<semver> per release.
 * debian/latest - this branch: debian/ recipe only, no source. Tagged
   debian/<version>-<rev> per packaging release.

The two branches never share files. A release build fetches source from
the v tag and lays this debian/ recipe over it. The payload is the
whole source tree copied to /opt/camlab, mirroring install.sh, so both
install paths produce identical trees.

Branch layout
-------------

 * debian/             - recipe (debhelper, arch:all).
 * .github/workflows/  - build.yml (shared build, workflow_call),
                         ci.yml (branch validation) and release.yml
                         (tagged publish).

Unlike the driver repos, this branch is deliberately detached from
Kurokesu/ci while app packaging stabilizes: workflows carry their own
build, sign and publish logic and call nothing remote. The sign step mirrors
deb-sign.yml conventions exactly (bundle naming, tilde rename, signed
SHA256SUMS), so apt archive ingest cannot tell the difference. Once
proven, the flow gets upstreamed into Kurokesu/ci as an app-family
reusable workflow.

Publish semantics also differ from the driver repos: deb assets land
on the existing v-tag source release, next to the README-pinned
camlab-rpi-<version>.tar.gz. The publish step deletes only assets it
owns (SHA256SUMS* and camlab-rpi_*.tar.gz bundles), never the source
tarball, and touches no release flags or notes. The Kurokesu/apt
manifest PR is what gates the archive.

Releasing
---------

A release is a pair of tags:

 * main is tagged v<semver>, revision-less, e.g. v1.0.0. Cut per
   RELEASING.md on main, which also creates the GitHub release deb
   assets land on.
 * debian/latest is tagged debian/<version>-<rev>, carrying a Debian
   revision, e.g. debian/1.0.0-1. Pre-release '~' becomes '_' in the
   tag (illegal in git refs), e.g. debian/1.0.0_beta.3-1.

To release, after the source release exists:

 1. Open a debian/changelog entry whose upstream version matches the
    source release in Debian form ('-' pre-release separator becomes
    '~'). Commit, push, wait for green CI.
 2. Tag this branch with debian/<version>-<rev> and push the tag. That
    triggers release.yml: paired-tag preflight, build against the
    v tag, sign, upload onto the v-tag release.

A packaging-only rebuild (1.0.0-1 to 1.0.0-2) is a new changelog entry
and a new packaging tag. The source tag is reused, not retagged, and
the publish step replaces the previous revision's bundle.

Release assets: a tarball camlab-rpi_<version>.tar.gz carrying the .deb
plus the build's .changes and .buildinfo, alongside SHA256SUMS and a
detached SHA256SUMS.asc signed with the archive key. A source package
stays in workflow-run artifacts only.

Setting up a box
----------------

The package owns /opt/camlab and /usr/bin/camlab-setup, a symlink to
install.sh in the payload. Everything outside /opt, the service, boot
config, sudoers and the read-only overlay, is camlab-setup's, run once
after install. That work asks the operator questions and writes
/usr/local, neither of which belongs in a postinst.

install.sh skips its copy step when it runs from /opt/camlab, so the
same script serves both the package and a clone.

Migrating from an install.sh install
------------------------------------

install.sh copies the repo to /opt/camlab unowned by dpkg. postinst
removes files under /opt/camlab the package does not own, so a first
dpkg -i over a tarball install converts the tree cleanly and stale
modules cannot shadow packaged ones.

The /usr/local/bin/camlabctl symlink stays install.sh-owned. Debian
policy keeps packages out of /usr/local.

Forks
-----

Updates only apply where the installed camlab-rpi came from the
configured archive origin, so a git fork or a tarball install gets no
update path. A fork wanting its own updates either renames the package,
declaring Conflicts and Replaces on camlab-rpi since both own
/opt/camlab, or serves camlab-rpi from its own archive.
